#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093357 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the area of the region bounded by the polar curve $r = 3\\cos\\theta$ and the rays $\\theta_1 = 0$ and $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown in blue in the picture below. Can we calculate this area using polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/2c/2c7e638a338aed2155ef5bf388be1f83a3a67d2296d5be09987b099a07edacbd.png)\n\nIn general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the half-circle above can be obtained as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "37d5627c-6e4c-5ffe-b3f4-9efac9c1cc1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1126 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093452 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2{:}$\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo see where the formula comes from, we can think about breaking up the area bounded by a polar curve into infinitesimally small sectors of circles.\n\nAs shown in the graph below, each sector has a radius of $r$ and spans an infinitesimally small angle, $\\textrm d \\theta.$\n\n![](images/42/42cac6a68ea519fb52c82fffa79d64f02e5cf7bbc2450ae964618ee2dd25827e.png)\n\nThe ratio of the area of a sector to the full area of the circle must be the same as the ratio of the angle of the sector to a full revolution of the circle. So, if the area of a sector is $\\textrm dA,$ then we can set up and solve the following proportion:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dA}{\\pi r^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\theta}{2\\pi} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\textrm dA = \\dfrac{1}{2} r^2 \\textrm d\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the area under the curve, we can integrate the areas of all the sectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba36924-1af3-515d-ac60-aab46240a7e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4582 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"}]}
